From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:30:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: do what the Messaging app asks: edit, delete, groups,
 places, contact cards and mentions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

* Edit a sent message when the Messaging app asks

The Messaging app can now edit a message the user sent. Its command
reaches imlibpurpleservice, which emits webos-im-send-edit for every
account with the chat, the message's serviceMessageId and the new
text. The first handler that returns TRUE takes the command.

Connect to it, with the reaction signal, once for the whole process.
For a Telegram account the handler takes the chat and message ids
from the serviceMessageId ("<chatId>:<messageId>", as the plug-in
gives one to each message it shows). It turns the text into plain
text as send_im does and asks TDLib to editMessageText. It returns
FALSE for another plug-in's account, for an account not connected, for
an id it cannot read, for a chat it does not know, and for a text that
is not one plain message, so the transport can log a command no one
took. If TDLib refuses the edit, the chat says so. A successful edit
comes back as updateMessageContent, which the plug-in already shows.

The parsing of the id is in webos-send.cpp, without TDLib or
libpurple, so it can be tested on its own. The test mock now keeps
what is connected to a signal, so a test can call it, and gives an
account the protocol id it was made with.

* Delete a message for everyone when the Messaging app asks

The Messaging app can now delete a message. imlibpurpleservice emits
webos-im-send-delete for every account with the chat and the
message's serviceMessageId.

For a Telegram account, take the chat and message ids from the
serviceMessageId and ask TDLib to deleteMessages with revoke set, so
the message goes for everyone in the chat. TDLib (1.8.67) checks
whether a message can be deleted for the others only for bots: for a
user account it deletes the message here and hands the request on to
Telegram, revoke and all. The deletion
comes back as updateDeleteMessages, which the plug-in already shows.
If TDLib refuses, the chat says so. The handler returns FALSE for
another plug-in's account, an account not connected, an id it cannot
read and a chat it does not know.

* Create a group when the Messaging app asks

The Messaging app can now start a group chat. imlibpurpleservice emits
webos-im-group-create for every account with the group's name and its
members, buddy names joined by "\x1f".

For a Telegram account, find each member's Telegram user the way
send_im finds whom a message is for (by "id<n>" buddy name, phone
number, display name or username), and ask TDLib to
createNewBasicGroupChat with them. The handler returns FALSE, without
creating anything, if the name is empty, if there are no members, or
if any member is not exactly one known user: a group without someone
the user picked is not what was asked for. A member named twice is
added once.

The new chat comes as updateNewChat and a first "created the group"
message, which give it a conversation as any group chat gets one, so
the app has a thread at once. TDLib answers with the chat and those it
could not add (for their privacy settings, say); they are named in the
new chat. If TDLib refuses the group, an error notice says so.

The existing createGroup, behind libpurple's join-chat dialog, is left
as it is: it finds members by display name only and ignores TDLib's
answer.

* Add members to a group when the Messaging app asks

The Messaging app can now add people to a group chat. imlibpurpleservice
emits webos-im-group-invite for every account with the group chat's
purple name and the members, buddy names joined by "\x1f".

For a Telegram account, find the group, which has to be one we are a
member of, and each member's user the way send_im finds whom a message
is for. Add them to a supergroup with one addChatMembers, and to a
basic group with one addChatMember each (TDLib's addChatMembers is for
supergroups and channels only), forwarding none of the earlier
messages, as the plug-in's libpurple invite does. The handler returns
FALSE, adding no one, for a chat that is not such a group, for no
members, and for a member that is not exactly one known user. Those
added show up as updates to the members and messages of their joining;
those TDLib could not add, and any error, are told in the group.

addChatMember answers with failedToAddMembers, not ok, since TDLib
023ee0971 (1.8.27 development). The libpurple invite took that answer
for an error and said "Cannot add user to group" after a successful
add; take either.

* Leave a group when the Messaging app asks

The Messaging app can now leave a group chat. imlibpurpleservice emits
webos-im-group-leave for every account with the group chat's purple
name.

For a Telegram account, find the group, which has to be one we are a
member of, and ask TDLib to leaveChat. The handler returns FALSE for
any other chat. Our leaving comes back as a change of our membership
and a message of it; an error is told in the group.

This is leaveChat alone. leaveGroup, behind libpurple's "leave", also
deletes the chat's history and takes it off the chat list. The app
keeps the thread after leaving, and the user deletes it there if they
want to.

* Send a location as a location, a named one as a venue

The Messaging app can now share a place. It sends an ordinary message
whose text is a plain form of it ("📍 Name https://maps.google.com/?q=
lat,lon"), and imlibpurpleservice sets "webos-send-location" =
"<latitude>\x1f<longitude>\x1f<name>" on the conversation just before
serv_send_im or serv_chat_send, and frees it as soon as that returns.

Copy it in send_im and chat_send, as the data is gone afterwards, and
never free or clear it: it is the transport's. When it holds a
latitude and a longitude in range, send an inputMessageLocation, or,
when the place has a name, an inputMessageVenue with the name as its
title and no address, provider or id, which TDLib accepts. A reply
target the transport gave goes with it. When the data is not a usable
location, or not there, the text goes as before, so a client that
cannot show a place still gets one.

What is sent this way is carried, with the reply target, through the
private chat created when there was none yet; the reply target was
lost there before.

The parsing is in webos-send.cpp, with g_ascii_strtod, which reads
"50.45" whatever the locale's decimal point.

* Send a contact card as a Telegram contact

The Messaging app can now share a contact. It sends an ordinary
message whose text is the card's name and numbers, and
imlibpurpleservice sets "webos-send-contact" to the vCard (3.0, with FN
and TEL lines) on the conversation around serv_send_im or
serv_chat_send.

Copy it in send_im and chat_send, never freeing or clearing it, and,
when the card has a phone number, send an inputMessageContact instead
of the text. The number is the first TEL; the first and last name come
from N, or, where it has no given name, all of FN goes as the first
name, or the family name, as TDLib's contact has the first name to
show. The whole card goes along as the contact's vCard. If someone on
Telegram has that number, as far as the plug-in knows the users, the
contact names them, so the other side can write to them from it; the
number is compared by its digits alone, and never when it has none, as
a user whose number we do not know has an empty one. A card without a
number goes as its text.

The card is read in webos-send.cpp: folded lines, grouped properties
("item1.TEL"), quoted parameters, backslash escapes, and the "tel:"
URI vCard 4.0 may give.

* Make the "@name" mentions of an outgoing message links

The Messaging app can now mention a member in a message. The text has
"@<name>" where each mention goes, and imlibpurpleservice sets
"webos-send-mentions" on the conversation around serv_send_im or
serv_chat_send: records "<id>\x1f<name>" joined by "\x1e", the id being
the mentioned buddy's name.

Copy it in send_im and chat_send, never freeing or clearing it. Find
each mentioned user the way send_im finds whom a message is for, find
each "@<name>" in the text TDLib is given (after markup is stripped and
the message split, in each part), and give it a textEntityTypeMentionName
entity for that user. The text stays as it is, so it reads the same to
a client that makes nothing of the entity. TDLib counts an entity's
offset and length in UTF-16 code units, which is how they are counted
here: an emoji outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is two. A mention
of someone not exactly one known user, or whose "@<name>" is not in
the text, gets no entity.

A mention is looked for after the one before it first, then anywhere,
so two people of the same name each get their own; and "@Ann" is not
taken from "@Anna" where there is a "@Ann" that a word does not go on
from. This, the records and the UTF-16 count are in webos-send.cpp.

* Take someone out of a group when the Messaging app asks

The app's removeFromGroup reaches the plug-in as the transport's
"webos-im-group-remove" signal (account, the group's purple name, the
members "\x1f"-joined), as an invite comes as "webos-im-group-invite".
The members are named as the group's member list names them: "id<n>"
for a buddy, else the display name.

Each member is set to chatMemberStatusLeft with setChatMemberStatus,
as libpurple's /kick already does. TDLib (1.8.67) takes a member out
of a basic group with the server's messages.deleteChatUser, and out of
a supergroup with a short ban that it lifts a second later, so that
they may be added again or come back by an invite link.
banChatMember would keep them out of a supergroup for good, and delete
all they wrote there.

A member who is not one known user is left out, as are we ourselves
(that would be leaving, a command of its own); the others are still
taken out, and the signal is not taken if no one is left. Why one
could not be taken out (no rights, not a member) is told in the group,
and logged by the user's and chat's ids, without their names.

Unit tests in test/webos-commands-test.cpp.

